\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The special-unitary matrix of a unit quaternion}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{For the right complex structure of \ref{fdip-007G}, order the
basis #{(1,-j)}. Left multiplication by #{a+bi+cj+dk} then has
matrix
##{M(a+bi+cj+dk)=
 \begin{pmatrix}a+bi&c+di\\-c+di&a-bi\end{pmatrix}.}
The standard Hermitian product on these coordinates pulls back
along #{M} to the quaternion norm, because the proof of
\ref{fdip-007G} gives #{M(q)^{\dagger}M(q)=|q|^2\bbI}.}

\p{The quaternion generators #{i,j,k} and #{(-1+i+j+k)/2} of the
tetrahedral preimage preserve the 4 imaginary vectors
#{(1,1,1)}, #{(1,-1,-1)}, #{(-1,1,-1)} and #{(-1,-1,1)}.
The first generators change two coordinate signs, and the last
cyclically permutes coordinates.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Quaternion.unitaryEquivSpecialUnitaryGroup}
formalizes the group equivalence between unit Hamilton quaternions
and #{SU(2)} by this matrix.}
